>1316</fpage><lpage>1319</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8567578</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B41"><label>41</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oe</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>de Fijter</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name><name><surname>Oe</surname><given-names>PL</given-names></name><name><surname>Stevens</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>de Vries</surname><given-names>PM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Diameter of inferior caval vein (VCD) and bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA) for the analysis of hydration status in patients on hemodialysis</article-title><source>Clin Nephrol</source><year>1998</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>38</fpage><lpage>43</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9710345</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B42"><label>42</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oe</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>De Fijter</surname><given-names>WM</given-names></name><name><surname>De Fijter</surname><given-names>CW</given-names></name><name><surname>Straver</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Oe</surname><given-names>PL</given-names></name><name><surname>Stevens</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Detection of hydration status by total body bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA) in patients on hemodialysis</article-title><source>Int J Artif Organs</source><year>1997</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>371</fpage><lpage>374</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9298408</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B43"><label>43</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jaeger</surname><given-names>JQ</given-names></name><name><surname>Mehta</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Dry weight and body composition in hemodialysis: A proposal for an index of fluid removal</article-title><source>Semin Dial</source><year>1999</year><volume>12</volume><fpage>164</fpage><lpage>174</lpage></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Bland-Altman plot between the mean values of measured and estimated REE and difference between measured and estimated REE using (A) HBE, (B) WHO, (C) Schofield, (D) Mifflin, and (E) Cunningham equations in all study subjects (n = 38). The 3 horizontal lines represent + 2SD, mean, and - 2SD of the differences between measured and estimated REE values. HBE, REE by Harris Benedict equation; Mifflin, REE by Mifflin equation; WHO, REE by WHO equation; Schofield, REE by Schofield equation; Cunningham, REE by Cunningham equation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-255-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Correlation between measured REE and HBE in total patients, normohydrated patients (open circle) and overhydrated patients (closed circle).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-255-g002"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Equations Used to Assess REE</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-255-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>BW, body weight; Ht, height; LBM, lean body mass.</p><p>HBE, REE by Harris Benedict equation; Mifflin, REE by Mifflin equation; WHO, REE by WHO equation; Schofield, REE by Schofield equation; Cunningham, REE by Cunningham equation.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Patient Characteristics</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-255-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>DM, diabetes mellitus; HTN, hypertensive nephropathy; CGN, chronic glomerulonephritis; PKD, polycystic kidney disease; SGA, subjective global assessment; REE, resting energy expenditure; adjREE, REE adjusted by lean body mass (kg).</p><p><sup>*</sup>Number of episodes of CAPD peritonitis from the start of CAPD to the beginning of the present study.</p><p>To convert hemoglobin in g/dL to g/L, multiply by 10, to convert Bun in mg/dL to mmol/L, multiply by 0.357, to convert creatinine in mg/dL to mol/L, multiply by 88.4, to convert albumin in g/dL to g/L, multiply by 10, to convert cholesterol in mg/dL to mmol/L, multiply by 0.02586.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Agreement between Measured and Predicted REE in All Patients (n = 38)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-255-i003"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>R, Spearman's correlation coefficient.</p><p>Limits of agreement: mean &#x000b1; 2SD.</p><p>RMSE = &#x0221a;{&#x003a3; (predicted-measured)2}/n.</p><p>r, correlation coefficient between the mean values of measured and estimated REE and difference between measured and estimated REE on Bland-Altman plot.</p><p><sup>*</sup><italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05 vs WHO and Schofield.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>